{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786969131,"nonce":"ZabtDLnlJTt/2WRDZqdisUpLk2s=","title":"`login.tsx` centres the ensō at 112px and stops, so the one screen §M.2 permits atmosphere on has none","message":"**The demonstration.** Open `/login` on a fresh install. The ensō lockup renders at 112 px in `text-line-strong`, centred above a 320 px form, on an otherwise empty canvas — `web/src/routes/login.tsx:88`. The comment above it (`:75-87`) argues correctly that this is the one screen with no incident on it and therefore the only one that gets the mark itself, and that the negative space around it is doing as much work as the ink. Both sentences are true, and the screen still stops at a centred glyph on a flat cream field. The mark is present; the atmosphere the comment describes is not.\n\n**Why it matters.** This is the first screen an operator sees and the only one where §M.2 permits anything at all — every other surface has an alert on it, and decoration there costs a firing row its scarcity. The design study's conclusion was that the ensō should bleed off two corners at `--oto-wash`, behind the form, with the form left bare rather than plated. Bare matters: putting the form on an opaque `bg-surface` card is the trivially safe way to guarantee contrast and it turns a threshold into a card, which is the opposite of what the `:75-87` comment is asking for.\n\nThe contrast guarantee comes from geometry instead of from a plate, and this is the part worth getting right once. A second mask layer — a horizontal gradient transparent across the middle 400 px at any viewport width — makes the ink **geometrically incapable** of entering the form's column. Nothing to tune, nothing to eyeball, and it holds at every breakpoint without a media query. The alternative, picking an opacity low enough to look safe, is how §M.4's measured pairs quietly stop holding: the first study computed `--oto-text-subtle` dropping from 4.90:1 to 4.37:1 under a flat 6% wash, which fails AA, and nothing in CI would have caught it — `contrast.test.ts` measures token pairs, not composites, and the axe row that would see it is the one **UNWRITTEN** entry in §M.7.\n\n**Where.** `web/src/routes/login.tsx:74` (the wrapper that needs `relative overflow-hidden`), `:75-87` (the comment this ticket is making true), `:88` (the mark), `:90-91` (the 320 px form column the carve-out is sized against); `web/src/components/Logo.tsx` (72 KB of inlined path data, route-scoped to login today — the wash wants the same art as a `mask-image` from `web/public/motifs/`, not a second inline copy); `assets/logo/oto-logo-mono.svg` (65.7 KB, pure vector, no embedded raster — it is the source); `docs/design/SPEC.md:4892-4920` (§M.2, why this screen is the exception); the `--oto-wash` token and `@utility` from the primitive ticket.\n\n**Scope.** small — one wrapper, two positioned spans, one mask layer. It depends on the primitive.\n\n**Done when.** The door carries the ensō bleeding off two corners at `--oto-wash`, the form stays bare on the canvas with no plate, and the ink cannot reach the form's column at any viewport width because a mask layer forbids it rather than because an opacity was chosen carefully. The art is referenced as a mask from `web/public/motifs/` rather than inlined a second time. Nothing on the screen animates — U9's decorative one-shot budget is already spent by the fūrin's 180 ms greeting (ADR 0028), and a fading wash would be a second one.","files":null}]}